<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candaules</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 680 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candaules was the last king of the Heraclid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, killed around 680 BCE by Gyges, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how Candaules, proud of his wife's beauty, made his bodyguard Gyges see her naked, and how the queen then forced Gyges to kill the king and marry her. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> preserves a different legend, the ring of Gyges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candaules.jpg" alt="Candaules" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Etty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Etty_(1787%E2%80%931849)_%E2%80%93_Candaules,_King_of_Lydia,_Shews_his_Wife_by_Stealth_to_Gyges,_One_of_his_Ministers,_as_She_Goes_to_Bed_%E2%80%93_N00358_%E2%80%93_Tate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
